From 64c5dd1cbdea299a8ee7712f319780bf9f94acb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu Duponchelle <mathieu.duponchelle@opencreed.com>
Date: Tue, 16 Jul 2019 01:02:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sctp_output: fix non-FreeBSD compilation (#324)

Fixes an issue introduced in f4166b347188af0de05fa70456042ef8a792cdd7,
the scope must be closed outside of the FreeBSD clause, as it is opened
outside of it.

diff --git a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
index 713a304..d0e8f27 100755
--- a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
+++ b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
@@ -13868,8 +13868,8 @@ sctp_lower_sosend(struct socket *so,
 		if (flags & MSG_EOF) {
 			sinfo_flags |= SCTP_EOF;
 		}
-	}
 #endif
+	}
 	if (sinfo_flags & SCTP_ADDR_OVER) {
 		if (addr)
 			net = sctp_findnet(stcb, addr);
-- 
2.25.1

Code statements:
```c
		if (flags & MSG_EOF) {
			sinfo_flags |= SCTP_EOF;
		}
	}
```

Code statements:
```c
		if (flags & MSG_EOF) {
			sinfo_flags |= SCTP_EOF;
		}
-	}
 #endif
+	}
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
#if defined(__FreeBSD__)
		if (msg->msg_flags & MSG_EOF) {
			sinfo_flags |= SCTP_EOF;
		}
	}
#endif
```
Reason:
The closing brace for the FreeBSD-specific section was incorrectly placed, which caused the logic for handling `MSG_EOF` to be scoped only within the FreeBSD conditional. This resulted in the flags not being set correctly for non-FreeBSD platforms, leading to compilation issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
	}
#endif
	if (sinfo_flags & SCTP_ADDR_OVER) {
```
Reason:
The patch moves the closing brace for the FreeBSD conditional outside of the conditional block, ensuring that the logic for handling `MSG_EOF` is correctly applied to all platforms, thus resolving the compilation issues for non-FreeBSD systems.

